    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most homeowners the real comparison is not sealer brand against sealer brand. It is a weekend and a couple hundred dollars of materials against a contractor bill that is often two to three times higher. Both can be the right answer. The difference comes down to driveway size, how many cracks need work, and what your time is worth.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer, in Dollars
          </h2>

          <p>
            Doing it yourself, materials for an asphalt driveway typically run somewhere around $0.10 to $0.30 per square foot for two coats, depending on the sealer you buy and how porous the surface is. Hiring a residential sealcoating company usually lands around $0.20 to $0.50 per square foot, with many companies setting a minimum job charge that applies to small driveways regardless of size. These are broad national ranges at the time of writing; local prices vary.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges at the Time of Writing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$0.10-$0.30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DIY materials per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$0.20-$0.50</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Contractor per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$150-$300</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common contractor minimum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical reseal interval</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Put those numbers against a real driveway. A two-car driveway of about 800 square feet might cost roughly $100 to $250 in sealer and filler if you do the work, plus a one-time $30 to $60 for a squeegee and a mixer paddle. The same driveway hired out commonly comes in somewhere around $200 to $450, and more if there is significant crack repair or the crew needs to haul equipment a long way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those totals assume an asphalt driveway in reasonable condition that mostly needs cleaning, some crack filling, and two coats. A driveway with widespread cracking, potholes, or sunken areas is a different job, and the repair work can cost more than the sealing itself. Sealing also only makes sense on a driveway that is old enough; paying to coat asphalt that went down this summer wastes money and can trap oils the pavement still needs to release.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of this guide breaks those ranges into line items so you can price your own driveway, decide which route is cheaper for you, and tell whether a contractor quote is reasonable or padded. If you have already decided to do it yourself, our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> covers the work itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Doing It Yourself Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer is the biggest single expense. Retail 5-gallon pails of water-based asphalt emulsion sealer commonly sell for roughly $25 to $60, with the low end being basic emulsions and the upper end being rubberized or polymer-modified formulas with longer label warranties. Each pail typically covers around 250 to 400 square feet per coat, so the cost of a two-coat job depends heavily on whether your driveway drinks material or lets it spread.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pails Needed (2 Coats)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Cost Range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Total With Filler and Tools</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400 sq ft (1-car, short)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50-$240</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$90-$330</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">800 sq ft (2-car)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-7</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$100-$420</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$150-$520</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,200 sq ft (long or wide)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$150-$600</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$210-$720</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000 sq ft (rural or circular)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-16</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$250-$960</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$320-$1,100</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The table uses a coverage range of 250 to 400 square feet per pail per coat and the retail price range above, so the low numbers pair a tight surface with budget sealer and the high numbers pair a rough surface with premium sealer. Most real driveways fall in the middle. Always buy one extra pail and return it unopened if you do not need it; running short partway through causes visible lap marks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crack filler adds roughly $10 to $25 per jug or a few dollars per caulk tube. A neglected driveway can easily use three or four jugs. An oil spot primer is another $10 to $20 if you have drip stains. On the tool side, a squeegee-brush combo, an edging brush, painter's tape, and a mixing paddle for a drill add up to roughly $30 to $70 the first time, and most of that is reusable on the next round.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hidden DIY cost is time. Expect most of a weekend for a two-car driveway, including cleaning, crack work, two coats, and cleanup. If you would otherwise pay someone to do it, that weekend is where the savings come from. If a pressure washer rental or a trip to the dump for old pails is involved, add those too. Our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a> include budget and premium picks so you can price both ends.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contractor-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Sealcoating Contractors Charge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Residential sealcoating companies usually price by the square foot, then apply a minimum charge so a small job still covers travel and setup. That minimum is commonly somewhere around $150 to $300. Above the minimum, many quotes fall in the $0.20 to $0.50 per square foot range for cleaning plus two coats. Crews using spray rigs can coat a driveway quickly, which is why per-foot rates often drop on larger jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crack sealing is often priced separately, either by the linear foot or as a flat add-on. Professional crews typically use hot-pour rubberized crack sealant melted in a kettle, which bonds and flexes better than most retail cold-pour fillers. Rates vary widely by region and crack width, from under a dollar to a few dollars per linear foot. A driveway with a few short cracks might add $25 to $75; one with long, branching cracks can add a few hundred.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some companies sell multi-visit packages or discounts for neighbors who book on the same day, and spring and early summer are their busiest months. Booking in late summer or early fall, while temperatures are still in range, can sometimes get a better price or at least a shorter wait. Be cautious of anyone going door to door with a leftover load offering a same-day deal; that is a classic setup for a thinned product or a poor job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT A FAIR QUOTE INCLUDES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cleaning, crack treatment, the sealer type, the number of coats, and the cure time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A quote that names only a total price and a date leaves out the things that decide how long the job lasts. Ask for the product name or at least its base type, the number of coats, how cracks will be treated, and how long you will need to keep cars off. Reputable contractors answer those without hesitation.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Ask about a workmanship guarantee as well. Some contractors will come back to touch up peeling or missed spots within the first season. Few will guarantee results on a driveway that was already failing, which is reasonable. Get at least two or three written quotes, and compare them line by line rather than by bottom-line total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Factors That Move the Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size is the obvious one, but the relationship is not linear. Small driveways cost more per square foot because of minimum charges and the fixed time spent on setup, edging, and cleanup. Very large driveways often cost less per foot because spray equipment covers open stretches quickly. A long, narrow driveway with many edges, borders, and turns takes more hand work than a simple rectangle of the same area.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condition comes next. A driveway with oil stains needs degreasing and priming, and one with many cracks needs filler or hot-pour sealant. Badly oxidized gray asphalt absorbs more sealer, pushing material use toward the low end of the coverage range. If a contractor sees potholes or crumbling edges, expect a separate patching charge, and patching with hot mix asphalt costs considerably more than sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Product choice matters less than many people expect. The jump from a basic emulsion to a premium polymer-modified sealer may add only a few cents per square foot to a contractor bid, but it can extend the useful life of the coat. Coal-tar sealers are restricted or banned in a number of states and municipalities, so a contractor in those areas will quote an asphalt emulsion or acrylic product instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Region and season round it out. Labor rates in high-cost metro areas push quotes toward the top of the range, while rural and lower-cost areas often sit at the bottom. In northern climates the sealing season is shorter, so demand bunches up in late spring and summer. In hot southern climates the season is longer but crews may work early mornings to avoid flash drying on very hot pavement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-or-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When DIY Saves Money and When It Doesn't
          </h2>

          <p>
            DIY is clearly cheaper for a typical suburban driveway in decent shape. You can usually save a couple hundred dollars on an 800-square-foot driveway, and the tools carry over to the next round in two or three years. The work is physical but not technical: clean, fill cracks, spread thin coats. If you are comfortable spending a weekend on it and can keep cars off for a day or two, doing it yourself is hard to beat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring out starts to make sense when the driveway is very large, steep, or long enough that hand-squeegeeing becomes a two-weekend project. It also makes sense when the crack work is heavy. Professional hot-pour crack sealant generally outlasts retail cold-pour fillers, and contractors can do that work in hours instead of days. If you have physical limits or simply do not want sealer on your shoes, that is a fair reason too.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a middle path. Some homeowners hire a contractor for hot-pour crack sealing and then apply the sealer coats themselves. That puts professional work on the part that matters most for keeping water out, while keeping the cheaper DIY labor on the coating. It is worth asking local crews if they offer crack-only service; many do, especially outside peak season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever route you take, the fundamentals that decide the outcome are the same: a clean, dry surface, cracks filled first, weather in range, and thin coats. Our article on <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">the pros and cons of sealing asphalt</a> also covers when sealing is not worth paying for at all, such as a driveway that already needs resurfacing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Compare Contractor Quotes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put quotes side by side and list the same items for each: square footage, cleaning method, crack treatment and price, sealer type, number of coats, application method, and cure time. If one quote is much cheaper, it usually skips something, most often the second coat, the crack work, or the cleaning. A one-coat spray job can look good on day one and wear through on the traffic lanes by the next year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the square footage. Contractors sometimes estimate from the street, and a quote built on a larger area than you have will be inflated. Measure the driveway yourself and compare. Likewise, if quotes price crack sealing by the linear foot, ask for the estimated footage. That gives you a way to compare a $0.75 per foot quote against a $2 per foot quote that assumes fewer feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask how the crew handles edges, walkways, and the garage apron. Overspray on concrete, brick, or siding is a common complaint with spray rigs, and a careful crew masks or hand-applies near those surfaces. Ask what happens if rain arrives the day of the job, and confirm there is no extra charge for rescheduling. Reputable outfits will not seal on a marginal day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look for local reviews and a business address. Driveway sealing is a classic target for traveling crews who offer a deal on leftover material and disappear. A legitimate contractor will give a written estimate, name the product, carry liability insurance, and not ask for full payment up front.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-run" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Long-Run Math: Is Sealing Worth Paying For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing is maintenance, so the useful comparison is against the cost of replacing pavement early. Resurfacing an asphalt driveway with a new overlay or replacing it outright costs many times more per square foot than sealing. Even a modest extension of pavement life, a few years over the full lifespan, can more than cover a decade of sealing at DIY prices, though the exact benefit depends on climate and traffic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing every two to three years, rather than every year, is where the math works best. Annual sealing adds cost and can build up layers that crack and flake. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> explains how to tell when the surface actually needs another coat. Many driveways that still bead water after two years can wait another season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crack filling delivers the best return of any line item. Water entering through open cracks is what undermines the base and leads to potholes and settlement. If the budget is tight, spending on crack repair this year and sealing next year is a better order than sealing over open cracks. Our list of the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> covers the retail options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing is a poor investment on a driveway that is already failing structurally. If more than about a quarter of the surface has alligator cracking, or there are many potholes and sunken areas, the money is better saved toward resurfacing. Sealing over that damage makes it look better for a season but does nothing for the base, and the cracks will be back after the first hard winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Sealer by the Pail, Not the Square Foot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap pail that covers 250 square feet can cost more per foot than a pricier one that covers 400. Divide price by the label coverage figure before you compare. Then use the low end of the coverage range for an old, porous driveway, because rough surfaces soak up more material than the label assumes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Lowest Quote Without Reading It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cheapest contractor bid often drops a coat, skips crack work, or uses a thinned product. Compare quotes line by line on cleaning, crack treatment, sealer type, coats, and cure time. A quote that will not name the product or coat count is a warning sign, not a bargain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Crack Repair Out of the Budget</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer is the visible part of the job, but crack filling is what keeps water out of the base. Budgeting only for pails of sealer leaves cracks open under a fresh coat, and they reappear after one winter. Price the crack filler or hot-pour service first, then add the sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Every Year Out of Habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Annual sealing roughly doubles the long-run cost compared with a two-to-three-year cycle, and the extra layers can crack and peel. Do a simple water test each spring instead. If water still beads on the surface, the existing coat is still doing its job and the money can wait.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying to Seal a Failing Driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer cannot fix alligator cracking, potholes, or a sinking base. Paying for a fresh coat on pavement that needs resurfacing buys a season of better looks and nothing else. Get an honest assessment of the structure before spending on sealing, and put the money toward repairs if the base is the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to seal a 2-car driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A two-car driveway of roughly 600 to 900 square feet typically costs somewhere around $100 to $300 in materials if you do it yourself, including sealer, crack filler, and basic tools. Hiring a contractor for the same driveway commonly runs about $200 to $450, and more with heavy crack repair. Prices vary by region and by how porous the existing surface is.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many pails of sealer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the driveway area by the coverage figure on the label, then double it for two coats. Most asphalt sealers list roughly 250 to 400 square feet per 5-gallon pail per coat. An 800-square-foot driveway at 300 square feet per pail needs about six pails total. Use the lower coverage figure for old, rough, or very gray pavement, and buy one spare pail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is professional sealcoating worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be for large or badly cracked driveways, or when you do not have the time or physical ability to do the work. Contractors use hot-pour crack sealant and spray equipment that are hard to match at home. For a typical suburban driveway in good shape, though, careful DIY work with quality sealer usually performs similarly for much less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are some sealcoating quotes so cheap?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very low quotes usually skip something: the second coat, the crack sealing, a thorough cleaning, or full-strength product. Some traveling crews also water down sealer to stretch it. Ask each contractor to put the product name, number of coats, crack treatment, and cure time in writing, and compare those items rather than only the total price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing a driveway add value to a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealing does not add measurable resale value the way a kitchen remodel might, but a dark, crack-free driveway improves curb appeal and signals that the property has been maintained. Sellers often reseal a few weeks before listing for that reason. The real financial benefit is extending the life of the pavement and delaying the much larger cost of resurfacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I budget for sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on resealing roughly every two to three years for most driveways, and use a water test to confirm before you spend. Driveways in harsh sun or heavy freeze-thaw climates may need it on the shorter end; lightly used driveways in mild climates can sometimes go longer. Sealing every year is usually unnecessary and adds cost without adding protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest way to seal a driveway properly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean the driveway thoroughly yourself, fill cracks with a quality retail filler, and apply two thin coats of a mid-priced water-based asphalt emulsion with a squeegee. Skipping the cleaning or crack work to save money is false economy because the coat will fail sooner. Buying mid-range sealer and doing the labor yourself is where most of the savings are.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

